          "secNumber": "1.2",
          "heading": "Mathematical practice across the five lessons",
          "content": "Start with a toy example small enough to solve or check analytically. Define whether the variables are dimensionless, molecular counts, concentrations, rates or graphical quantities. When a mathematical property such as convergence or an optimum is asserted, write down the assumptions making that statement true. Compare at least two parameter regimes or alternative models rather than treating one attractive image as sufficient evidence. A plausible-looking simulation should be followed by a limiting-case calculation, a units audit and an explicit statement about which parts are fictional.\n\nThe worked cases associated with this unit are deliberately distinct: exposure area, background-corrected reporter, boolean state count, timescale separation ratio, share of conserved regulator. Each calculation isolates one quantitative relationship. The point is not to make every biological process reducible to arithmetic; it is to show where simple algebra can detect an impossible explanation and where additional experimental information would be needed to determine a physical parameter. Check at least one result both manually and with an independent computational implementation.",
